#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130071} :activity/id #uuid "40540042-d3fd-5add-a52b-682e9eaa6e90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048986 :topic/id #uuid "d11c4451-1bcb-5f1a-a6d3-3f5fdbe85bb7" :topic/math-academy-id 1997} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130399 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\color{black}0}^{\\color{black}2} \\int_{\\color{black}x/2}^{\\color{black}1} \\, e^{y^2}\\,\\textrm{d}{\\color{black}y} \\, \\textrm{d}{\\color{black}x}.\n$$\n\nThe expression \"$\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x\\,$\" tells us that we should integrate first with respect to $y.$ This is problematic, though, as the integrand $e^{y^2}$ does not have an elementary antiderivative. So, what can we do?\n\nIn cases like this, **reversing the order of integration** from $\\, \\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x\\,$ to $\\, \\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y$ might help. But beware! Because the domain of integration is non-rectangular, we can't just swap the order of the integrals. This is because, to integrate with respect to $x,$ we can't have $x$'s in the limits. So we need to work out new limits.\n\nWhen swapping the order of integration, it is helpful to sketch the region $D$ over which we're integrating.\n\n![](images/d3/d3ee76083ae3917b4585fce7227475d3e0471550f193f2fa437be3ebf92dc288.png)\n\nTo integrate first with respect to $y,$ we imagine a line parallel to the $y$-axis, as shown above. Note the following:\n\n- This line *enters* the region through the curve $\\color{black} y=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ and *leaves* at $\\color{black} y=1.$ These are the variable limits for $y.$\n\n- The minimum value of $x$ is $\\color{black}0,$ and the maximum value of $x$ is ${\\color{black}2}.$\n\nSo, the integral we were initially given is over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\left \\{  (x,y) \\: : \\: {\\color{black}0} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{black}2}, \\quad {\\color{black}\\dfrac{x}{2}} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{black}1} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we now want to integrate first with respect to $x,$ we need the limits for $x$ to be functions of $y$, and we need the limits of $y$ to be constants. To work these out, we redraw the region, but this time we imagine a line parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/c8/c80ae995a92aba4f535b5e5a01305f8ffbf790ad1f97ee8db4f368e4bfabb0c1.png)\n\n- The line *enters* the region at $\\color{black}x=0$ and *leaves* the region at $\\color{black} x=2y.$ So these are our variable limits for $x.$\n\n- The minimum value of $\\color{black}y$ is $\\color{black}0,$ and the maximum value of $y$ is ${\\color{black}1}.$\n\nSo, we can consider $D$ to be the following type II region:\n\n$$\nD=\\left \\{  (x,y):\\quad {\\color{black}0} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{black}2y}, \\quad {\\color{black}0} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{black}1} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressed the region $D$ as a type II region, we're ready to swap the order of integration and finally calculate our integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{1}\\,{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y{e}^{{y}^{2}}dy \\\\  & ={[{e}^{{y}^{2}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ccfd99d2-4e17-53c3-8ac5-2394c59be5c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4357 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e13e6db5-65c2-53ea-9644-faae6ec26f16" :step/math-academy-id 26536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129189 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129008 :question/id #uuid "9b2415da-21b8-5a59-9b3f-2f30b624c36f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7194"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488189}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "d45a7233-e073-55c6-8a71-375178f2f11a" :step/math-academy-id 26537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129009 :question/id #uuid "3477b5ca-309c-5b2a-b3fd-41dfc9bbf4db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7195"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049003}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488199}] :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"} :step/id #uuid "981fea3c-a71a-5567-bcaf-63176b8545f5" :step/math-academy-id 26538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130072}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129010 :question/id #uuid "eab89095-f5e5-53dc-9141-0f1a876f8a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7196"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475945}] :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"} :step/id #uuid "768398f5-6108-549e-9288-fd51dc694028" :step/math-academy-id 26540 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130073}}] :activity/title "Changing the Order of Integration in Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129342}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488297} :activity/id #uuid "bf5c1cdc-1231-5945-ae88-c7df3fc76214" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048986 :topic/id #uuid "d11c4451-1bcb-5f1a-a6d3-3f5fdbe85bb7" :topic/math-academy-id 1997} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab8fcd9-4602-5070-8690-3160b7afea7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487621} {:answer/id #uuid "295507dd-328a-5bb3-964f-3c0f304a9d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487650} {:answer/id #uuid "335927a5-38d1-5363-b0b7-58dbc7940dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(1-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487664} {:answer/id #uuid "6465b0aa-6d02-553e-acdc-7035e0eb70ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487768} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f6c3ee-d01c-5d14-87c0-fce9876c9b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487650} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eba9098-a15f-5710-a728-b6dfa4ffcdb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "18010b03-c75c-5a16-8ddc-495cf577e016" :question/math-academy-id "q-38799" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/54/54727a51e88b3543726b61606a0f41b9a8cf5ec46ef4c69d7233fd54a4b50242.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/95/956aaf81973d9a2b3e56082ecae8e654eee55d6f99c42dd433d126632d7c7262.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{4}}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{{y}^{4}}}{3}[{x}^{3}]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[{e}^{{y}^{4}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1e312c9f-cb74-5f7f-9c13-ab33fcd2a8b6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13af14b5-15a1-566b-a656-d8a378d7320c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324147} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1a2f03-596f-5e6f-978b-605dcec2e3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324215} {:answer/id #uuid "860bc5a9-e2eb-5f7c-a636-43aba8e0881d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{4-y}^{y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325106} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fec665-57db-5f83-bb6e-f9267a2d10aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{4-y}^{y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325336} {:answer/id #uuid "f2804b9b-bae3-55b0-8ce1-7ac7c4d428a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326107} :answer-field/id #uuid "759c1086-e737-5e21-a10a-cebde7f9cab4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "51957ec1-4df6-5139-b864-fabbb3776e09" :question/math-academy-id "q-38601" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea36f1ec1ddeb03dd8d133fb8d65b5d50b3fa07ba34495978d87f95742dee042.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-x\\,⟹\\,x=4-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e82098a4bb63f1d709a06b6a24da078594716d6a3cc0fa2e069af9dcd9154955.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32fd6dca-607a-5214-a4a3-fbde7a7bf2c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1215436d-cf83-5f92-b39e-a9bec379f2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y/2}}{\\int }_{(4-y)/2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487602} {:answer/id #uuid "1291e1c1-cc7f-5dab-8331-0c5c3605b71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487605} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce71b5a-ae72-56f3-907b-b226cfb92e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{(4-y)/2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487782} {:answer/id #uuid "a34f841b-c478-5350-b05a-4956614fb33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487872} {:answer/id #uuid "a838ae42-337e-55e8-9ec4-76976d1219db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc9b43e-629a-5bba-b51a-3bf554da7329" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "291bba76-b18a-519c-8c81-930cc19f299e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82253" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/83/8359a189981b238d0851324a896ed0a3a9aa0620e8cf4d38228752ff484b4cd0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2{x}^{2}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2(x-2)^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{4-y}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\\le x\\le \\frac{4-y}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/bee5de8ca9011672a159686d1547a909e5288097b15241f5004e5c788459ee70.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2(x-2)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48d26477-5711-5c8c-9f6f-58692fb49ba5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12862e2a-8176-5480-9c3a-b73349d4eada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487603} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfe33b9-51d1-5788-8662-58bc8ab68510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487684} {:answer/id #uuid "469a96e1-ead2-518e-bb41-99b1ce5ab6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487704} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfda9e5-2b1f-5351-b880-190a9511ab54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487811} {:answer/id #uuid "ff35cb83-3ce5-547e-a2d3-554a83f4034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487704} :answer-field/id #uuid "acad3c64-a048-5588-ba2a-180649362504" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1781d10d-4447-567d-8dfd-9f16c098a159" :question/math-academy-id "q-132647" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d2/d2ef7f656cf706636c82e1ef41ae338a596fff5142962a74875f762301937932.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le y\\le -1,\\,1\\le x\\le -\\frac{4}{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{y}\\,⟹\\,y=-\\frac{4}{x},\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{4}{x}\\le y\\le -1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/33/3338ee0f8bdc6f18bb5a56b12a36d31d79c87db6c39686adc7ae7c00efb9d526.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}[{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-4/x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{x}-1)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+2}-2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1+2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2}-2 \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "66d3bee5-2c83-50d6-baeb-1f27e2bd6de0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "092a29e0-d74d-5d43-953c-1f5d9bf2a5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388295} {:answer/id #uuid "60cc9f6b-b572-50b9-952f-12e386a01c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388296} {:answer/id #uuid "aea1d3dc-a5bf-519d-8f6d-ded54403dbfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388297} {:answer/id #uuid "133a0e32-0ea5-5f11-8c08-78665b249cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388298} {:answer/id #uuid "81d539a4-fe56-54af-a346-d28ee3e104cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388295} :answer-field/id #uuid "66942a40-bd6e-5606-91e2-d8c0f63814d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9a7843d-3020-5482-8aef-e872811d9165" :question/math-academy-id "q-132565" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/34/3426e7bdd134bc9af5a3665f8c9c55a65514afa9dca5be112eabb37479c4c9ec.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1-e\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\le x\\le 1\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le e-1,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\le x\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,⟹\\,y={e}^{x}-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\,⟹\\,y=1-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1-{e}^{x}\\le y\\le {e}^{x}-1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7d/7d88d351b481859ec3e77350d3679387836d2e7f67325caca50e2f61cce9dcce.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a31ece2d-f9c7-54ca-957f-b6feaf1a1054" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488310}}] :activity/title "Changing the Order of Integration in Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487562}]}
